{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Stdio MCP Server Boundary.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Stdio MCP Server Boundary.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"stdio mcp server boundary stdio mcp server boundary","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Stdio MCP Server Boundary.md","fileName":"Stdio MCP Server Boundary.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Stdio MCP Server Boundary","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 12:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[DevOps Agent Memory and MCP]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["DevOps Agent Memory and MCP","agenticaifordevopsengineers.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Stdio MCP Server Boundary.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Stdio MCP Server Boundary.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"stdio mcp server boundary stdio mcp server boundary","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Stdio MCP Server Boundary.md","fileName":"Stdio MCP Server Boundary.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Stdio MCP Server Boundary","heading":"Stdio MCP Server Boundary","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A stdio MCP server runs as a local child process and exchanges JSON-RPC protocol messages with its client through standard input and output. This is suitable when an agent and tool server run in the same CI job because no remote service is required.\n\nStandard output must be reserved for protocol traffic, so diagnostics go to standard error. The server owns the API token, request construction, and external side effects; the client receives only declared results. Child-process environment inheritance should be narrowed in production so unrelated credentials are not exposed to the server.","tags":["baby"],"links":["DevOps Agent Memory and MCP","agenticaifordevopsengineers.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Fu